WEBVTT

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:48.600
For three days each summer, Britain has a gathering unlike any other - a celebration of country sport that draws visitors in their tens of thousands.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:55.240
Hunting, shooting, fishing... ..conservation and kayaking too - it's all here at the CLA Game Fair.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:06.560
The Country Land and Business Association's annual game fair is one of the biggest events in the rural calendar.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:12.480
This year, it comes from the magnificent Harewood House in West Yorkshire.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.680
It is quite a setting.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.800
We'll be soaking up the atmosphere and going behind the scenes... ..bringing you a flavour of what's on offer.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.840
I'm hoping my dog, Oscar, can pick up some skills like these.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.320
'But it's not looking good.'

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:34.080
Whilst I'll be joining the ladies... ..showing the men how it's done.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.320
Complete natural.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.280
Also, Tom's in the majestic Scottish Highlands.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:51.000
Huge swathes of this wild country have been in the same families for generations, but dramatic changes to land ownership could change all that.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.720
But will it help Scotland's rural communities?

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.360
I'll be finding out.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.400
And Adam is also here in Yorkshire.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:06.720
I'm on the trail of Yorkshire's county breeds and these local beauties are known by some as the world's favourite pig.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.720
But what made them so popular?

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:29.360
This is the CLA Game Fair 2015 - a true celebration of the British countryside.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:41.800
If you're interested in horses and hounds, country crafts, fishing, falconry or ferrets, this is the place to be.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.640
We are in the grounds of Harewood House in west Yorkshire, just eight miles north of Leeds.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:59.680
The site here on the Harewood estate is huge, but then it has to be, because this is the biggest moveable countryside event in the world.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.600
Amazing!

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:21.120
This year's game fair spans across 500 acres of the estate, and they are expecting to welcome 150,000 visitors over the weekend.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.280
The planning and preparation for this started more than 18 months ago, and yet the whole build is done by just 26 people.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:36.080
And to keep things running smoothly, you need people like Tony - and here is the main man himself.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.640
Tony, how are you?

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.320
Hello, Anita.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.080
How are you?

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.520
I'm fine, thank you.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.520
The organiser of the whole affair.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.600
How is it looking today?

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:42.920
It's looking...

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:52.080
When I consider we had 40 hours of rain between Sunday and Tuesday, and I don't have an enormous team, I have to say, it looks absolutely fabulous already.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.640
How long has the game fair been running?

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.480
58 years - it started with a budget of £850.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.200
I would be frightened to tell you how much it is now.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.480
No, go on, tell me.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.080
It's certainly well over...

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.120
It's close on £5 million to put the show on.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.920
Wow.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.160
What goes on at the game fair?

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.520
It's a celebration of the countryside.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.200
So it is guns, it is fishing, it is shopping.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.600
We become...

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:23.400
I think it's the seventh largest retail area in the country for this weekend.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.840
It's a real celebration of the culture of the countryside, isn't it?

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.560
You don't have to be have a 4x4 and a wardrobe full of tweed to come here.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.120
No.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.680
Although there is a heck of a lot of tweed here!

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.080
There certainly is.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.840
And on a hot day, I do admire anybody who wears it.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:44.520
We encourage all walks of life to come to the show, because that is something for everybody, I really do...

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.920
I'm not saying that because I'm talking to you.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.480
It really is true.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.200
Have a "gander" at this!

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.600
'And, yes, before you write in, I know they're ducks.'

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:07.440
There is so much going on down here, and overseeing it all is that magnificent house just over there.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.080
Joe is having a quick look inside.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:21.240
Harewood House is incredible - 181 rooms of exquisitely crafted, restored and preserved history.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.440
This 18th-century house was designed, built and decorated by the absolute best of the best at the time.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:34.000
Now, this is the gallery - it's nearly 80 feet long and isn't it a magnificent space?

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:47.360
Now, local Thomas Chippendale designed all the original furniture here at the house and, if you look above the windows and you see these ornate curtain designs, they were actually made by his son.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.960
But, incredibly, they are not made of cloth - they are actually carved from wood.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:04.600
There are three libraries in the house, together holding more than 11,000 books.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:09.480
This one is the Spanish library, complete with Chippendale steps - well, it wouldn't be a library without them, would it?

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:19.520
The walls around the house are adorned with wonderful art, with some pieces painted especially for the family.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.800
Just look at this beautiful painting of the house.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:30.560
It was painted only 20 years after the house was built and it's an incredibly romantic vision.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:31.560
And guess who the creator was?

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:51.280
It was a young Turner, who was relatively unknown, just at the start of his career and, if you come with me, you can see the very hill where he would have been, in this beautiful landscape - of course, designed by Capability Brown - which, today, is housing the game fair.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.840
And if you have the eyesight of the game fair falcon, you might just be able to pick out Anita somewhere down there.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.400
I'm just here, Joe.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:00.360
Working very hard... having ice cream.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.200
The incredible gardens really are the perfect backdrop for the fair.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.480
The view is magnificent - as is the ice cream.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:18.640
As you'd expect, there are landowners here from across the country enjoying the fair.

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.760
But, as Tom has been finding out, those from Scotland may have other things on their minds.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:34.800
Scotland - for centuries, people battled for ownership of its vast and varied landscapes.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.000
The great Scottish clans fought with each other and the English over land.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:49.080
The crofters' claims to land were literally swept away by the Highland Clearances of the 18th and 19th centuries.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:55.800
Who owns and uses the mountains, hills and fields here has shaped the history of Scotland.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:02.400
And now, the battle cry can be heard again in Edinburgh, home of the Scottish Parliament.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:10.000
The SNP has set its sights on big landowners and wants to open up their private world to the masses.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.120
..Give effect to our commitments.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:15.520
The government intends to embark on a radical programme of land reform.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:22.200
Presiding Officer, Scotland's land must be an asset that benefits the many, not the few.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.400
So, who are the few?

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:33.919
Well, just 432 individuals own half of Scotland's private land.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:42.520
The SNP and their leader, Nicola Sturgeon, thinks that is outdated and unfair so they're planning to change it with a Land Reform Bill.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:51.040
But before getting into the details of the bill, I need to understand the background.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:54.280
I'm heading to the Hebridean island of Eigg to find out.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.760
Colin Carr has been a tenant farmer for more than 25 years.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:06.560
He farms sheep and beef, whilst wife Marie runs a bed and breakfast.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:13.480
'He used to run the farm in partnership 'with a former landlord, 'but their relationship turned sour.'

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:24.680
His circumstances changed, and then he actually wanted the house we were running...staying in and running as a bed-and-breakfast...

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.040
He wanted that back.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:32.120
And if we didn't comply...basically, then he tried to terminate the farming partnership as well.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:40.160
'Although he was never evicted from his home, 'Colin felt he couldn't plan for the future 'or invest in his business.'

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:45.880
It was quite difficult, because there was no security - you didn't really know how long you were going to be here or how many...

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:48.120
What difficulties you were going to have with him.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.160
So it all got a bit bad, really.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:10:00.200
Stories like Colin's are the reason why the Scottish government has proposed sweeping reforms.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:17.080
They include improved rights for tenant farmers, removal of tax exemption for shooting and deerstalking and, in extreme cases, ministers could actually force the sale of land.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:25.280
And that has enraged landowners, some even likening it to Zimbabwe, when farmers were forced off their land.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:29.120
'I put this to the SNP's Richard Lochhead.'

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.920
So, is this a Mugabe-style land grab?

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:41.320
People who oppose land reform usually have a vested interest, and they tend to be the people who talk about land grabs - no-one else is talking about land grabs.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:49.800
This is about fairness, equality, giving communities and, indeed, in some cases, perhaps tenant farmers, individuals working on land, the opportunity to have a better life, more say over their own future.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.960
It's about the government having the power to take land away...from owners.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:11:02.680
What the legislation proposes is under certain circumstances, where there's clearly no other option that will move things forward, the government, yes, will take the opportunity to intervene.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:08.920
Fighting talk from the SNP.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:12.840
But communities taking control is nothing new.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:23.480
Look at the island of Eigg, where I met Colin earlier - back in the 1990s, residents got so fed up with the succession of absentee landlords, they ended up buying their island.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.960
The price tag - £1.5 million.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:45.960
Eigg could be the poster child for the SNP's land reforms - they want a million acres under community ownership, just like this, by 2020 and the government plan to put aside £10 million to encourage similar community buyouts.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:49.720
It certainly worked out for farmer Colin.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:58.920
He's now got a permanent lease with the island trust, which has given him enough financial security to refurbish his rented home.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:05.720
And you wouldn't have done this kind of work under the old regime.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.200
Not a chance.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:13.440
They wouldn't have invested in it and we wouldn't have had the confidence to go in like we did with the trust.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:13.880
Yeah - oh, it's great.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:30.040
The islanders of Eigg have been able to invest in a new ferry terminal to boost tourist numbers, and the latest in solar and wind power, making the island virtually self-sufficient in electricity.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:36.760
Lancashire lass Maggie Fyffe moved to Eigg in the late 1970s, becoming one of the founders of the island trust.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:42.320
People want to develop the island and, you know, the various aspects of it, but they're not being given the chance at the moment.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.480
What do you think about the proposals that are now in front of the Scottish Parliament?

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:53.840
I think anything that makes it easier for communities like us to do what we've done is all to the better, it really is.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:13:01.080
You know, a good landlord has nothing to fear from the legislation.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:06.440
People might look at this and think it's all very easy - is it?

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:06.800
No, I wouldn't call it easy!

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:14.240
No, it was really, really hard work and it's hard work...you know, keeping the whole show running.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:23.560
Survival hinges on the finances and some buyouts have struggled to make ends meet.

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:29.960
The island of Gigha was transferred to the community in 2002, but hit the headlines when they were dealing with debts of nearly £3 million.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:33.040
They're managing, but it's a potential warning to others looking to do the same.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:40.720
So, do tenants have the skills and experience to take over from their landlords?

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:53.400
Far from being absent or lazily looking down from their castles, many landlords say they are preserving their estates and the communities within them.

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:57.000
So, should we be keeping the status quo for the sake of rural Scotland?

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:57.920
That's what I'll be finding out later.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:06.960
Today, we are at the CLA Game Fair on the Harewood estate near Leeds.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:23.760
When you wander around a fair like this, one that is so focused on game hunting, you don't naturally think about wildlife conservation, but I've been told that the two can go hand-in-hand.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:31.720
Roger Draycott is a biologist from the Game And Wildlife Conservation Trust.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:35.840
Roger, surely people here are more interested in shooting game than preserving it?

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:49.200
Well, really, Anita, if you're interested in shooting pheasants, then to run a successful peasant shoot, you have to be managing all the habitats that the pheasants need really well, otherwise the shoot simply won't be successful.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.800
And how would you do that?

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:59.440
Well, in woodlands, that is managing the understory to create this nice, shrubby cover, that lots of other birds are going to benefit from, but then if you move into the farmland, then we tend to grow lots of crops like this.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:02.920
Right, I know what this is - this is quinoa - because I like to eat it.

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:04.040
Well, I would call this quin-o-ah!

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:13.320
A wonderful crop to provide cover for pheasants and partridges in the winter and it also provides an abundance of seed food as well.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:21.560
It produces lots of feeding plants for pollinators, things like butterflies, bumblebees, honeybees, that we all know are suffering as well.

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:25.320
The goal is for more habitat like this.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:38.440
So I'm leaving the crowds behind to head for a quiet spot on the estate, where an amazing transformation is taking place.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:41.440
It is a wild corner that has been turned around by Christopher Usher.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:47.120
Like his father before him, Christopher is the estate manager here at Harewood.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:50.440
It is so beautiful here, Christopher.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:52.880
It feels like we've entered another world.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:55.080
We have, I suppose, in many ways - in two ways, really.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:16:05.480
We're on the north-east side of the estate on one of our arable farms down at Stockton and in terms of another world, you are right in one context, because when I was a boy, all this was desert sort of farming - huge areas of growing...

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:15.400
Where we are standing now, it was growing corn, whereas now, as you can see, we've got a pond here, a pond there, bulrushes, we've got fantastic, diversified habitat.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:20.920
All the hedgerows you've seen around here are new, the forestry strip going over there...

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:21.800
So these trees are not...

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.280
They haven't been here for centuries?

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:24.920
Absolutely not.

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:28.960
They're probably 15 years old, part of the Farm Woodland Premium Scheme when they were planted.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.680
It's hard to imagine it was anything other than this, because it feels so natural - it works.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:40.960
It makes a wildlife corridor and a link - by creating this habitat, and the incentives on the back of the shoot doing a lot of that, we've got this great area back again.

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:52.480
I think that's where I'm slightly conflicted - it's this idea of all of this wonderful conservation work to bring back the wildlife and create this fantastic natural habitat for then to...shoot it and kill it.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.720
But look wider - I think we should need to look wider than shooting.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:00.080
We've invested in our barn owl population, there's a barn owl box, to the right here.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:12.280
We've got a great brown hare population here, plus, of course, all the songbirds that benefit, the pollen and nectar mixing here, the beetles and other insects and amphibians and everything else that will be here, too, on the back of it.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:18.599
Everyone benefits and we perhaps shoot 40 ducks a year from it and enjoy eating them.

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:23.200
So not every bird on the estate is there for the guns.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:30.760
Christopher and his team are working to increase the numbers of one of our most endangered wild birds - the grey partridge.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.480
I suppose it wouldn't be Yorkshire if we didn't have a bit of rain.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:47.160
We've lost 90% of our only native partridge in the last 100 years, mostly due to loss of habitat.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:54.640
'Harewood's head gamekeeper, Tim Rothwell, 'is trying to buck that trend 'and he's called upon some unusual help.'

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:59.320
So, you've got quite a special way of feeding these grey partridges, haven't you?

00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:59.640
What do you do?

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:04.240
That's right - we use broody hens.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:04.680
Banties, mainly.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:07.920
The hens are essentially fostering the grey partridges, aren't they?

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:12.960
Doing it like this, I can get more chicks from one hen partridge.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.680
There was three pairs on the estate when I came, just on one farm on the estate.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:16.400
And now?

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:20.080
We've got 15 pairs, and that is right across the estate as well, so...

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:25.240
You've increased the numbers fivefold, but 15 pairs still doesn't seem like many, does it?

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:29.360
And you've got lots of chicks here in these runs, so what happens to them?

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:32.440
Some of those will migrate onto neighbouring farms.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:35.120
There's a saying - you've got to stock your neighbours before your own estate gets stocked.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.760
For every hundred you put out, you might only keep five pairs on the estate.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:43.360
So once you've got the numbers that you're looking for, what happens to the birds?

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.960
Are they there to be shot?

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.080
No, they're not, that's not the purpose of the project.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:50.120
It's just our way of getting a few back on the estate, back into the countryside.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:52.040
Do they have an identity crisis?

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:56.520
Do they grow up thinking they're hens and not partridges?

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:59.960
They create their own little family units, called a covey.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:06.280
So in this coop here, we've got some that are about three weeks old.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:09.280
They're all ready to be run through into the A-frame, so they'll have a bit more room.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:16.000
There comes the first little one.

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:17.600
Come on...ooh!

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.760
Once one goes through, you'll get...the others will follow.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.680
The hen is beautiful.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:33.720
And they're in.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:36.240
Right - so, they'll have a great time for a few more weeks.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:37.160
Until they go out into the big wide world.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:40.800
They just need some nice weather as well.

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:44.200
Well, it's Yorkshire, so...

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:47.760
They'll be hardy birds anyway, reared in Yorkshire.

00:19:52.120 --> 00:20:00.920
It's been all over the news in recent days that one of the big issues in farming is the crisis in the dairy industry.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:07.480
We've been following one young farmer to find out what falling milk prices mean to her and why she decided to take action.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.840
I'm Becky Robertson.

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:16.720
I run a small family dairy farm in south Gloucestershire.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:24.440
We have 100 Holsteins and the milk price is threatening my farm.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:31.320
For 26-year-old Becky, her first year as a dairy farmer has been tough.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:38.840
But while many struggling farmers have been forced to leave the industry, she is determined to have a future in a job she loves.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:51.320
We've got a couple of heifers in here which are due to have their first babies and then the rest are, sort of, second, third, fourth calvers and, basically, they're on a two-month holiday before they have their babies.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:55.520
We love our cows and, of course, we want to survive.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:21:08.640
We've gone from 32p down to 21p within a matter of a year and if this price carries on or gets even lower, like there's talks of it happening, there won't be a dairy future ahead of us.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:17.800
It costs Becky around 26p to produce a litre of milk and that's despite investing heavily in more cost-effective production, including the latest robotic milking machine.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:27.840
This is one bit of technology that robots have - the camera that attaches to the units, which then films the udder to detect where the teats are in order to connect the unit.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:38.720
This is laboursaving, as we don't have to get involved in putting the units on ourselves.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:45.200
I feel that this farm is the most efficient that we can get to.

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:49.120
These robots, there isn't much that they don't pick up.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:22:00.040
I know which cows haven't come in to be milked, I can tell how much they're milking each day, I can tell if they've got mastitis before signs start showing...

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.160
It's a very high standard of efficiency.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:06.600
We aren't in this industry to make lots of money.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:13.760
A year ago, this business would have been sustained, not a problem, with the price of the milk.

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:17.000
Unfortunately, since then, it's only come down.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:20.480
All we're asking for is to make a living and look after our girls.

00:22:21.560 --> 00:22:27.400
Exactly who is to blame for the current dairy crisis is far from clear-cut.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:44.560
A dramatic drop in global prices has certainly had a major part to play, but for producers like Becky, British retailers and dairy processors make a real difference when they pay farmers more for a litre of milk.

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:48.920
To encourage them to do just that, farmers have been campaigning hard.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:54.480
Becky has been involved in something called The Milk Trolley Challenge, and videos of their efforts have gone viral.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:56.680
It seems to have had an effect.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:09.240
The Milk Trolley Challenge is basically a group of farmers, or people supporting our industry, going into a supermarket that isn't paying fairly.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:12.880
We take the milk completely off the shelves.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:23.640
We then leave them in the trolleys, either dotted around the store, in front of the counters - we made a point of putting it in front of the water, as water is more expensive than milk.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:27.960
That way, they were getting the message that we weren't happy with what prices they were selling our milk for.

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:41.560
The public's response has been fantastic - we've had support in the stores, we've had people videoing us, we've had people coming up to us saying they would be prepared to pay more for their milk, and that is exactly the response we were after.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:53.840
In the past, there has been many protests about the milk price, and I think that the reason we have captured the public eye is because the younger generation have started this.

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:59.000
The main thing is that we've now had a response from the supermarkets, which is what we wanted to achieve.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:05.200
They've now started talking to us, negotiating - we've had a step in the right direction.

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:26.440
Being new into the dairy industry and...from, obviously, the younger generation, it is...it is quite scary in the current situation we're in, whether I have got a future in farming.

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:37.800
I don't want to see these girls go, and I will keep fighting for them, but unless the supermarkets help and the price we get paid goes up, unfortunately, I can't see that being a bright future ahead of us.

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:47.880
So, would you pay more for your milk if that money was guaranteed to go to the farmers?

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:57.320
Let us know via our website... ..or share your thoughts on Twitter, using...

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:03.400
And you can follow us...

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:10.360
In Scotland, Tom's been looking at plans to change the way land is owned and managed.

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:13.040
He has already met the people who welcome the reforms.

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:16.320
Now, he's off to see those who aren't so keen.

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:30.600
From the Highland glens, down to the fertile Lowlands, half of Scotland's private land is owned by a tiny minority.

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:35.160
The Scottish government doesn't think that's very fair.

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:42.120
And, with the help of a controversial Land Reform Bill, they want to put more land in the hands of more people.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:47.600
But many landowners and even estate workers think that could do more harm than good.

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:58.480
Landowners like Jamie Williamson - or, rather, the laird of Alvie and Dalraddy estate.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:09.400
His family have owned these 13,000 acres for nearly a century, but the reforms could potentially force landowners like him to sell up to local communities.

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:11.160
Well, it's not a bad spot you brought me to.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:17.320
So what, for you, is the significance of places like this?

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:22.760
Well, my family has owned and invested in this land for over 80 years.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:23.320
Mm-hm.

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:28.000
So...and it's been very much a means now of making a living.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:39.600
The oldest trees were planted in 1866 to 1877, felled in the 1980s, and we've replanted it and it'll be another 80 years before we get a full return from the trees.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:42.360
So, the young trees, like the one behind me here, who will see a return from that?

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:45.400
Probably my grandson or my great-grandson.

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:53.000
Generations of Jamie's family have invested in this estate, ensuring its survival.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:56.760
What problem do you have with the idea of communities having a right to buy?

00:26:55.960 --> 00:27:00.160
It very much depends how far the government goes on this.

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:08.800
Obviously, we are concerned if a local community can say, "Oh, we rather like what you've got, we want it, we'll have it."

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:20.560
And this, because we're doing investment in the long term, would be a huge disincentive for us to continue investing, if we thought at any time anyone can come along and say, "Oh, we rather like what you've got."

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:28.680
As well as selling timber, farming and shooting, like many estates, Alvie and Dalraddy had to diversify.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:39.840
'The latest problem in Jamie's caravan park 'is in the toilet block, 'and this laird isn't afraid to get his hands dirty.'

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.440
So, being a laird is not all tweed and glamour.

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:45.360
No, no - there is an awful lot of clearing blocked drains, etc, as well.

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:50.000
'Jamie is passionate about the job 'handed down to him by his forefathers.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:53.480
'Under the reforms, he will probably hold on to his land.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:58.240
'But because he gets income from shooting, 'he's likely to be hit in the pocket.'

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:03.320
At the moment, sporting estates are exempt from paying business-rate tax.

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:07.480
The Land Reform Bill will put an end to that.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:18.400
'100 miles across the Cairngorms in Glenesk, 'fourth-generation gamekeeper Garry MacLennan 'earns a living from deer and grouse shooting.'

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.720
There you go.

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:27.840
'He's worried the plans to remove rate relief 'on sporting estates like his could hit hard, 'affecting the workers too.'

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:30.320
You know, the margins are very tight.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:31.600
Quite often, they run at a loss.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:33.960
The first thing that will happen is jobs are going to go.

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:34.520
So that's the real worry.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:37.880
I've got a family to support, you know...

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:38.920
The knock-on effect of...

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:42.840
We've got a very small school in the area.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:49.120
There's six pupils at present, so if we start losing jobs, families will go, and then, ultimately, the school could go.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:29:01.800
The Scottish government haven't really thought it through - they've got this idea of, "Let's attack the rich", but really, they are affecting the working people that they're wanting to help.

00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:09.280
Today, it's the village Highland Games - a chance for the locals to get together for a bit of light-hearted competition.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:17.320
For Garry, like others on the estate, they don't feel the need to own their land in order to belong here.

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:28.320
You look at what's here today - you know, we're standing on land that's owned by the Earl, and look what it's getting used for.

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:37.480
So I think if you were to go and ask some of the people that are here, I think that they'll feel very much part of the estate and part of the community.

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:46.600
So, the SNP's vision of a fairer Scotland could be different to that of many rural communities, like the one here in Glenesk.

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:51.080
Is that not a concern for the minister leading the Land Reform Bill?

00:29:50.960 --> 00:30:00.080
Clearly, the communities will very much be in the driving seat - if a community wants to take over land, the community will be in charge of its own destiny.

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:09.480
But what we have seen is where communities have taken over land, jobs are created - not scrapped, they are created, and therefore it is good for the local economy.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:13.320
But some of these people feel more secure with the landlord they know, rather than a community that they don't.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:25.000
If existing landowners are doing a good job for the local community and there's a good relationship with the local community, we don't see an issue with that - what we're doing is equipping the government on behalf of the public interest, to intervene where things aren't working.

00:30:25.040 --> 00:30:34.400
If all goes according to plan for the SNP, the Land Reform Bill could become law within a year.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:45.560
These mountains and glens have seen their fair share of conflict over the years, and now, it's the landowners who feel they're under attack.

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:51.280
The battle lines have been drawn and, over the next year, rural Scotland is going to see some fiery debate.

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:06.800
I've stepped away from Harewood House and the hubbub of the game fair to enjoy a tranquil moment or two.

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:15.360
This is the Himalayan Garden and, at its heart, there's something rather special.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:22.600
The garden was restored a decade ago, overseen by the current Lord Harewood, David Lascelles.

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:33.600
In the 1930s, his ancestors sponsored plant hunters to bring back exotic species from Asia.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:37.040
Since the restoration, the garden has blossomed.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:39.760
But it's not a flower I'm here to see.

00:31:38.880 --> 00:31:42.120
David is my guide.

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:48.080
Wow!

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:52.080
This is really quite incredible - not at all what I expected to see somewhere on your estate.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:57.080
Well, this is a little bit of the Himalayas, brought to an English country garden.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:32:02.320
It's a stupa, which is a Buddhist monument, which you'll find all over the Buddhist Himalayas.

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:09.600
Essentially, it represents a Buddhist concept of enlightenment and how to achieve it, a path to enlightenment.

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:12.440
So we have the architectural shape, the outer form, if you like.

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:17.240
It's a hollow monument, made of a series of five chambers, each of which are full of worldly goods.

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:25.080
In the bottom one, clothes, musical instruments, agricultural tools traditionally in the Himalayas, an agricultural community.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:29.240
I put a camera in there, because I used to work in film and television.

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:34.120
And then, building up, each one is like a little shrine, I suppose, but sealed.

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:40.480
What is inside is what kind of gives...what energises the outer form.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:41.400
So tranquil - the perfect place to escape the crowds.

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:43.840
What's it like having a huge show in your back garden?

00:32:44.120 --> 00:32:47.800
Well, only once every few years, but it's great.

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:48.640
I love to see the place being used.

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:49.960
I come here quite often, one of my favourite spots.

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:52.920
It's always quiet, even when it's very, very busy, like it is at the moment, up by the house.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:53.400
It's always quiet down here.

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:59.480
Here, in this corner of a busy Yorkshire estate, the perfect place to find a little solitude.

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:14.400
While we're at the game fair, Adam is also in Yorkshire for the latest of his films on our county breeds.

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:23.080
It fascinates me how, throughout the country, there are breeds particular to individual places.

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:30.280
Our livestock has been shaped by the landscape it lives in and the way we farm it.

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:34.360
So, today, I'm getting a closer look at seven county breeds that are special to Yorkshire.

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:47.320
When I think about the traditional breeds of Yorkshire, this is what springs to mind - Swaledale sheep, up here, about 1,000 feet up, on the weather-beaten hills of the Yorkshire Dales.

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:57.320
In fact, there are 30 times more sheep in the Yorkshire Dales than people - more than half a million of them.

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:00.080
They're hardy creatures, used to being out in all weathers.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:03.440
'Roy Nelson has been a shepherd here all his working life.

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:08.960
'I've chosen to meet him on a day that shows why farmers here 'need to be tough, too.'

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:11.239
This is a good bit of Yorkshire weather, Roy, isn't it?

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:11.920
Aye - it just had to rain.

00:34:11.199 --> 00:34:15.600
We've a marvellous view down there, if it'd been clear, but...it isn't, so that's that.

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:19.960
Why is it that Swaledale suits this region so well, then?

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:23.840
Well, they're just hardier.

00:34:23.920 --> 00:34:33.199
Ours live out on t'top in winter, more or less same as they did 50 year ago, and grow to a size that they can support themselves.

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:37.199
So what should the ewe look like?

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:41.040
We like them with a good, hard coat on, a jacket and waistcoat...

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:42.520
LAUGHING What does that mean?

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:47.000
It wants to be a thick bed of wool that'll keep the wind out.

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:52.080
And then it wants a bit of top on it to throw the water off, so as we're like today...

00:34:51.199 --> 00:34:53.840
Yeah, it just runs away.

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:59.080
And if you were out here all winter, the first thing you'd want would be a good, warm, dry top coat.

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:03.520
So, you've got these tough sheep up here - you've got to be a pretty hardy farmer too, haven't you?

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:06.160
I don't know - it's just what we're used to.

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:07.240
You just don't think about it.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:11.120
Do you feel the sheep are part of you, as a Yorkshireman?

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.880
Oh, definitely, yes.

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:22.640
One of the biggest thrills to me is when somebody buys one off me, and then they take it to a show somewhere and they'll maybe win at a show with it.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:23.480
I feel quite proud of that.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:36.520
And the annual Ryedale Agricultural Show is just the place to see the best of this breed, in amongst 1,000 sheep exhibits.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:40.280
Now, here we are - let me explain something to you.

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:57.600
That Swaledale ewe here, the female, not only is a fantastic sheep at surviving on the hills, but also, if she is crossed with this ram, the Bluefaced Leicester, this great big brute, put him on her, you get a lamb called a Mule.

00:35:57.080 --> 00:36:01.560
And this is a Mule ewe - a wonderful mother.

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:10.960
So, she has fine wool, lots of lambs, plenty of milk from the Bluefaced Leicester and then that wonderful mothering ability and hardiness from the Swaledale.

00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:23.160
It is an incredible mix that creates this fantastic sheep that is now the mainstay of lowland sheep farming, producing lamb for the table.

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:27.400
And that is why the Swaledale is the backbone of the UK sheep industry.

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:30.760
But Yorkshire is not just the birthplace of hardy sheep - it has some good lookers, too.

00:36:29.960 --> 00:36:35.760
You've heard of Wensleydale cheese - well, this is the Wensleydale sheep.

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:39.720
This one is called Nosterfield Lulu 2.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:45.840
She has just won Reserve Supreme Champion of Show for owner Ernie Sherwin.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:47.480
How long have you been breeding Wensleydales?

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:51.600
They've been in the family for over a century, really, over the years, with my grandparents and my dad and things.

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:52.360
So you feel really passionate about them as a breed.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:54.160
Yes, they're a bit of a fun thing.

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:57.200
I love to keep these traditional breeds on for their wool.

00:36:56.520 --> 00:37:00.960
The fleeces are worth in excess of £100, now, in the right places.

00:37:00.040 --> 00:37:01.840
Well, congratulations with her.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:02.560
Beautiful.

00:37:02.360 --> 00:37:07.480
They came from Wensleydale itself.

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:08.320
Yes, yeah.

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:13.680
The Cistercian monks are supposed to have set up with the Wensleydale creamery to start off milking them and making cheese, apparently.

00:37:13.120 --> 00:37:16.680
So that's where the famous Wensleydale cheese comes from.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:18.080
Yeah.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:24.160
There are also 1,000 horses on show here, and there's one local breed I'm particularly interested in.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:32.440
In the show ring now is the Cleveland Bay - they used to be known as "the Chapman horse".

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:41.840
The chapmen were the travelling salesman and they used these animals as pack horses, to carry all their wares around, and they were very versatile - they could pull the plough, they could be ridden or they'd pull carriages.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:44.240
But sadly, now they're in decline and they're a rare breed.

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:55.240
This is another classic Yorkshire breed - the Middle White, with its lovely, squashed nose.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:38:04.080
It was developed about 200 years ago by a guy called Joseph Tully in Keighley, West Riding.

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:04.880
They're great at producing pork.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:07.600
But there is another breed of pig from Yorkshire that's become famous worldwide.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:16.760
The Middle White's much bigger cousin is creatively called the Large White.

00:38:15.880 --> 00:38:24.360
They used to be seen as the perfect pig, sent all over the world to establish commercial breeding herds, earning them a great reputation.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:28.520
'They are still a favourite of Ron Fieldhouse, 'who breeds them on his farm in Tadcaster.'

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:33.280
Goodness me - how many piglets has she got?

00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:34.760
13.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:36.720
They are gorgeous!

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:39.320
Tell me about the breed itself.

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:40.400
Where did it originate from?

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:53.720
It originated in West Riding of Yorkshire and was quickly developed into a fast, lean-growing pig, suitable for the bacon trade and was exported worldwide.

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:54.680
And when was it at its height?

00:38:54.120 --> 00:39:12.440
I would say it was at its height in the '50s, and then started declining in the late '70s, when the breeding companies started developing hybrids, which are basically a mixture of Large White and other breeds.

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:13.680
Do you think they still have their place?

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:20.280
They still have their place, yes - they are still a fast, lean-growing pig that can compete with the commercial market.

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:23.800
We call them Large Whites, but worldwide, they're known as the Yorkshire pig.

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:25.920
Lovely - how does that make you feel?

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:27.440
Oh, very proud.

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:37.040
Despite the recent decline, it's hybrids of the breed that are used by the large-scale producers to supply supermarkets.

00:39:37.400 --> 00:39:39.640
They are big pigs, aren't they?

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:40.520
Yes.

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:43.000
'And here in Yorkshire, the purebred Large Whites 'are in safe hands.

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:47.040
'Ron's son David is building up the herd to help preserve them.'

00:39:49.600 --> 00:40:00.720
We've seen that there is going to be a real problem, because people just aren't breeding them, so there are a few of us that are just trying to get them out and get them spread throughout the country.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:05.320
Do you keep them because they're a good pig, or because they're Yorkshire pigs and you're a Yorkshireman?

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:05.680
It's a bit of both, really.

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:12.480
I mean, there is pride in it that they are the Yorkshire breed and we are based in Yorkshire, so what better pig to keep?

00:40:14.120 --> 00:40:20.440
Yorkshire's local breeds have spread their influence far beyond the farmyard.

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:30.000
Bred here in the 1860s for hunting, the Airedale terrier became a hero of the First World War.

00:40:29.640 --> 00:40:36.280
They were trained to find wounded soldiers and carry messages.

00:40:35.600 --> 00:40:40.280
'But it is one of the world's smallest dogs 'that is the best-known Yorkshire breed of all.'

00:40:40.320 --> 00:40:43.680
Why have you chosen to keep Yorkshire terriers?

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:49.480
Because they are a nice little dog - the hairs don't drop out, and they're...

00:40:48.680 --> 00:40:49.960
Come on!

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:51.960
They're sporting.

00:40:51.160 --> 00:40:56.760
She does the ratting and very good at ratting and this little one that's hanging back - come on, mate - I use her for my deerstalking.

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:04.200
She finds all the deer and the rabbits that I shoot.

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:04.720
Really?

00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:07.520
Proper little working dogs, then.

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:08.080
Proper working dogs.

00:41:07.200 --> 00:41:08.520
How did they come about?

00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:10.120
Why Yorkshire terriers in Yorkshire?

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:14.600
Well, they were bred initially for keeping the rat population down in the mills.

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:17.000
And there would have been a lot mills round here.

00:41:16.120 --> 00:41:17.400
Hundreds.

00:41:16.600 --> 00:41:19.240
Hundreds of mills, yes.

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:23.000
I suppose, as a little dog, they can get into all the nooks and crannies and...

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:23.520
Yes.

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:24.000
..And get in and catch rats.

00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:25.920
Lightning quick as well.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:30.720
They have got a bit of a reputation as being a handbag breed - have they been bred smaller for that?

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:34.840
Yes - an old lady's dog.

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:36.840
But they're not.

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:43.600
They will tackle any dog when they're out on a walk, with or without the ribbons in their hair.

00:41:42.840 --> 00:41:45.520
ADAM LAUGHS They've got a big attitude for a little animal.

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:46.000
They have got a big attitude.

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:49.520
They can be a bit snappy, can't they, sometimes?

00:41:49.560 --> 00:41:52.360
This one has never bitten anybody, yet...

00:41:52.720 --> 00:41:54.280
There's always a first time!

00:41:53.320 --> 00:41:54.560
Always a first time.

00:41:55.400 --> 00:42:06.000
Tiny dogs, enormous pigs, versatile work horses - Yorkshire has given us so much.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:07.200
But, for me, this is the image that defines the county.

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:14.400
A man, a dog, and a flock of Swaledale sheep - the romance and the grit of farming in Yorkshire.

00:42:19.920 --> 00:42:33.680
You probably get the idea by now - the CLA Game Fair really is the place to be for those passionate about country sports.

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:38.200
I really feel as though I've entered the belly of rural life.

00:42:37.360 --> 00:42:43.600
This is proper, tweed-wearing, hunting, shooting, fishing territory.

00:42:46.880 --> 00:42:52.760
The shooting line here is the longest in Europe, and it gets busy - at its peak, 4,000 shots each hour.

00:42:51.880 --> 00:42:56.840
There are competitions for the world's best, as well as a chance for anyone to come and have a go.

00:42:57.280 --> 00:43:06.600
Believe it or not, guns have never really featured in my life, but I've been challenged to a friendly competition by a local club.

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:09.280
Apparently, I'm just the sort of person they want to recruit, so I'd better get some practice in.

00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:17.840
Mark Windsor is a true sharpshooter - one of England's best.

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:23.760
He's got 20 years' experience under his belt, and it shows.

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:30.200
He also happens to be one of the best coaches in the country, so I reckon I'll be in safe hands.

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:39.560
OK - what we are going to do, before we do anything with this gun, before we fire it at all, we're going to do a dry run, OK?

00:43:38.680 --> 00:43:39.800
Sure.

00:43:38.840 --> 00:43:44.040
We are basically going to simulate what is going to happen when you actually use the live shot.

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:44.320
So we are live, now.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:44.920
OK.

00:43:43.960 --> 00:43:45.560
Pull.

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:50.520
OK, so follow it up to the middle - bang.

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:50.960
No!

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:51.600
That was all over the place.

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:52.560
It doesn't matter.

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:54.120
That's why we do a dry run.

00:43:53.320 --> 00:43:54.880
Take the safety off.

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:55.400
OK.

00:43:54.520 --> 00:43:57.080
It's fast, isn't it?

00:43:56.120 --> 00:43:57.240
Yes, it is pretty fast.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:44:00.320
But the one thing I will ask, OK, is you don't do too much movement with the gun.

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:02.320
Sure.

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:03.120
'Right - let's do this for real.'

00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:08.880
GUNSHOT OK.

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:09.240
Whoa!

00:44:08.360 --> 00:44:09.760
Well done.

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:10.840
Didn't hit anything - let's try it again.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:12.080
OK.

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:13.040
So, we're live.

00:44:12.600 --> 00:44:14.080
Pull.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:17.360
Here it comes - straight to the middle.

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:18.240
GUNSHOT Yes!

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:18.880
Lovely.

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:23.680
Within two shots, we have a broken target.

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:25.600
'Well, it's a good start.

00:44:24.880 --> 00:44:27.120
'But there's a way to go before I'm a true contender.'

00:44:28.520 --> 00:44:29.840
GUNSHOT Oh...

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:42.800
Look at that - absolutely fantastic.

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:45.040
GUNSHOT So, that was slightly left-handed...

00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:48.240
GUNSHOT Ooh...

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:48.680
So satisfying.

00:44:47.720 --> 00:44:49.240
GUNSHOT Yes!

00:44:48.280 --> 00:44:49.520
GUNSHOT Yes!

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:51.960
Well shot - brilliant!

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:52.200
Ha...

00:44:51.240 --> 00:44:52.800
Yeah.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:55.520
Well, I'm having fun.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:45:00.040
I think I need a little bit more practice before I enter this competition.

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:01.000
I can't believe I'm entering a competition.

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:02.160
You're doing really well.

00:45:01.360 --> 00:45:03.720
Why am I entering a competition?

00:45:03.160 --> 00:45:07.280
OK - so, I've busted a few clays.

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:09.000
GUNSHOT Perfect.

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:13.600
But back at the fair, there are some super sharpshooters.

00:45:12.640 --> 00:45:13.800
Pull - fire.

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:19.800
Wow!

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:22.920
Rachel, that was seriously impressive.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:23.640
Thank you.

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:25.600
I'm guessing this is no ordinary shooting club.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:28.400
No - we are Femmes Fatales, we are a ladies-only shooting club.

00:45:28.240 --> 00:45:29.720
Girls only?

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:30.480
Girls only.

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:35.200
Not the first thing you think about - I know this might sound sexist - but girls and guns?

00:45:34.280 --> 00:45:35.880
What's the appeal?

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:39.840
It's exciting, it's something social - we all get together.

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:42.760
There are so many girls that take part already.

00:45:42.040 --> 00:45:44.560
There's been a huge influx over the last few years.

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:45.880
It's our passion.

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:46.800
We love it.

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:48.800
Is this something woman can do equally as well as men?

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:54.800
There are no physical limitations as to why women can't be just as good, if not better, than men at shooting.

00:45:55.080 --> 00:46:00.640
It's not hugely physically demanding, anybody can do it - it doesn't matter what size you are, what age you are.

00:45:59.760 --> 00:46:05.520
Men and women, we can all do it together and, you know, shooting competitively, we all sort of compete on a level pegging.

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:12.320
And is it just...people who live in the countryside?

00:46:11.440 --> 00:46:14.920
Is that who shooting is for?

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:18.320
That is exactly the opposite aim of Femmes Fatales - it's just to appeal to everyone, from all walks of life.

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:25.040
It's brilliant to see, actually - it's not all tweed and flat caps.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:27.320
Right - the moment of truth.

00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:32.600
Three women, three clays, three shots each.

00:46:32.680 --> 00:46:34.320
It...is...on.

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:36.520
Three, two, one - pull.

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:43.120
ANITA LAUGHS Excellent - fantastic.

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:43.600
One down.

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:44.640
Pull.

00:46:47.800 --> 00:46:50.120
So far, so good.

00:46:49.400 --> 00:46:53.560
That's two out of two for me.

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:56.840
But that's a miss for the competition.

00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:57.040
And another.

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:01.840
Pull.

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:04.200
Oh, well done - she got it.

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:05.960
'So, it's down to my last shot.

00:47:05.000 --> 00:47:06.760
This is for the win.'

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:08.880
Exactly the same again, what you just did.

00:47:07.920 --> 00:47:09.200
Just do exactly the same again.

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:17.360
Amazing!

00:47:16.520 --> 00:47:18.560
Perfect shooting.

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:22.880
I can't believe that!

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:26.800
Thank you so much.

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:29.320
Three out of three.

00:47:28.520 --> 00:47:30.760
You are therefore the Femmes Fatales Countryfile Challenge winner.

00:47:30.600 --> 00:47:32.440
Congratulations!

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:35.920
Amazing - thank you, what an honour.

00:47:34.960 --> 00:47:37.400
You're an absolute natural.

00:47:36.440 --> 00:47:40.240
You've definitely got a future in competitive shooting.

00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:41.480
And you may well have another member.

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:43.200
We'd love to see you at our events - we'd love to have you back.

00:47:42.400 --> 00:47:43.520
Thanks for coming.

00:47:42.600 --> 00:47:44.200
It's been brilliant.

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:45.120
I love it.

00:47:48.280 --> 00:47:51.000
I can't believe I have got a proper little trophy.

00:47:51.400 --> 00:48:00.760
Now, clays are one thing, but if you're after game birds, as well as a steady hand and a good eye, it's a gundog you will need.

00:47:59.800 --> 00:48:01.440
Come on!

00:48:00.760 --> 00:48:01.880
Come on!

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:11.840
WHISTLE BLOWS Fiercely intelligent, energetic and bred and trained to obey, gundogs are masters of the shoot.

00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:17.320
Out in the field, they are essential, helping to flush out and retrieve game birds.

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:22.080
With the right handler, they can reach the top of their game.

00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:22.760
Which I'm not sure can be said of this pampered pooch.

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:25.960
Meet Oscar - he's our family golden retriever.

00:48:25.200 --> 00:48:28.080
We've had him since he was a puppy.

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:30.920
He's a bit eccentric, but he can be smart on his day.

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:34.680
I am just not sure he's gundog material.

00:48:33.800 --> 00:48:35.840
But I could be wrong and today, we're going to find out what he's capable of.

00:48:34.920 --> 00:48:36.000
Come on, mate.

00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:42.880
But first, I want to see an expert in action.

00:48:44.360 --> 00:48:49.200
So, I'm leaving the fair and heading to a nearby village, where I'm meeting someone who really knows her stuff.

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:56.760
She is one of the best young handlers in the country.

00:48:55.880 --> 00:49:00.880
She's put four gundogs through their paces and she even competes against adults.

00:48:59.960 --> 00:49:02.840
Meet Siobhan, who's 11 years old.

00:49:02.320 --> 00:49:03.480
Hey, Siobhan.

00:49:04.480 --> 00:49:05.560
How are you doing?

00:49:04.720 --> 00:49:06.400
OK.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:07.160
Who is this handsome chap?

00:49:07.520 --> 00:49:09.520
Sammy.

00:49:08.760 --> 00:49:11.120
Nice to meet you, Sammy.

00:49:11.480 --> 00:49:14.160
Talk me through what you do with Sammy, then.

00:49:14.120 --> 00:49:17.520
Well, my mum will throw a mark out.

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:18.440
I'll send him back.

00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:19.640
There it goes.

00:49:19.160 --> 00:49:22.320
Yeah, and, when I send him, he should go straight back and then come back.

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:23.640
OK.

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:35.200
So what are you doing with the whistle, now?

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:40.040
Well, that first whistle I did, that was my hunt whistle, and then the second one was my call-back whistle.

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:42.280
Good boy.

00:49:41.320 --> 00:49:42.480
Look at that.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:42.760
So, was that a good one?

00:49:43.120 --> 00:49:45.160
Are you happy with that?

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:45.440
Yeah.

00:49:44.480 --> 00:49:46.320
Does he do that for anyone?

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:50.240
Could I whistle and call him and he'd do it for me?

00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:54.880
No, he won't do it for my mum and he won't do it for my dad.

00:49:54.040 --> 00:49:55.560
Oh, really?

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:56.200
No, because he's like...

00:49:55.240 --> 00:49:57.080
He's a bit of a wimp, sometimes.

00:49:57.400 --> 00:50:00.440
He doesn't look like a wimp to me.

00:49:59.560 --> 00:50:01.280
Looks like a commanding, fine dog.

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:04.400
I tell you what, he'll make my dog look like a wimp.

00:50:03.560 --> 00:50:05.360
He's very good.

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:11.480
Siobhan will be going all out at the fair to add yet another trophy to her collection.

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:13.000
Good boy, Sammy.

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:13.480
Oscar, Come on...

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:15.560
Oscar and I have got quite a bit of work to do to get anywhere near THAT standard.

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:19.520
There's one person, though, who might be able to help us.

00:50:20.680 --> 00:50:22.680
Hello, Pippa.

00:50:22.920 --> 00:50:24.080
Hello, nice to see you.

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:25.320
And who is this young man?

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:28.720
This is Oscar.

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:33.080
I'm hoping you can be responsible for bringing the retriever out of him.

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:33.920
No, I'm a people teacher, Joe.

00:50:33.040 --> 00:50:34.840
I'm going to teach YOU.

00:50:33.920 --> 00:50:35.040
Oh, right...

00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:36.800
It's YOUR job to teach HIM.

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.280
OK.

00:50:37.680 --> 00:50:41.400
Well, I think we need to get him playing with his toy a little bit.

00:50:40.440 --> 00:50:43.120
So you want to keep it nice and jolly, nice and light.

00:50:42.600 --> 00:50:46.160
Just let him have a little play, let him catch it, and we'll just see what his response is to it.

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:50.080
You've got to be a bit more exciting than that, Joe.

00:50:49.200 --> 00:50:50.360
You've got to be much bigger.

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:53.920
Yeah, so you've got to run up and down.

00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:57.600
Oscar, come on.

00:51:02.480 --> 00:51:03.680
And, now, give him a sausage.

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:04.640
Lovely.

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:05.560
And let catch his toy.

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:06.120
Chuck it up in the air.

00:51:05.160 --> 00:51:06.320
Oscar.

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:10.720
Well, as they say, if at first you don't succeed, try, try again.

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:16.200
And again...

00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:16.440
And again.

00:51:20.320 --> 00:51:22.240
Oscar's gone to take his toy off.

00:51:22.760 --> 00:51:24.560
He's quite a selfish retriever.

00:51:25.720 --> 00:51:31.080
So, after a good half hour's instruction, we decide Oscar's ready for a retrieve.

00:51:30.240 --> 00:51:33.360
It's the job his breed is supposed to excel at, after all.

00:51:38.360 --> 00:51:39.440
Good boy!

00:51:38.480 --> 00:51:39.600
Well done!

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:39.880
Good boy!

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:40.200
Oscar, come.

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:40.600
Good lad.

00:51:39.640 --> 00:51:40.720
A flying puffin.

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:41.240
Oscar, come.

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:41.800
Well done.

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:42.160
Good lad.

00:51:45.560 --> 00:51:46.680
Not breaking any speed records.

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:47.240
Oscar, come.

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:48.480
Nearly.

00:51:48.200 --> 00:51:49.280
Nearly.

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:49.680
Oh, yes!

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:50.160
Well done.

00:51:49.200 --> 00:51:50.440
Superb.

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:52.200
And fast.

00:51:51.240 --> 00:51:52.480
Good boy.

00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:53.240
That deserves a bit of sausage.

00:51:55.520 --> 00:51:56.960
Good boy.

00:51:56.000 --> 00:51:57.360
Well done.

00:51:56.400 --> 00:51:59.480
How important is it to keep a dog like Oscar stimulated?

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:02.840
Oh, very important, because you need to keep him mentally as well as physically fit.

00:52:01.880 --> 00:52:03.000
So, what do you think?

00:52:02.440 --> 00:52:04.920
With a little bit of training, some potential there?

00:52:05.480 --> 00:52:06.920
A BIT of training, Joe?

00:52:06.320 --> 00:52:08.760
I think a lot of training on your behalf!

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:13.960
We have to put that work in, and then we get the response and we get the rewards come out of it.

00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:24.160
So, look, it's getting a bit late in the day for him, he's a bit lethargic, but we've seen some progress, haven't we?

00:52:23.240 --> 00:52:24.760
We've definitely improved on his recall.

00:52:24.120 --> 00:52:26.840
He's actually retrieving now, so we're getting there.

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:30.080
But how will Oscar do when we really put him to the test in just a moment?

00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:49.280
We're at the CLA Game Fair, one of the biggest events in the rural calendar, coming, this year, from the grand setting of Harewood House in West Yorkshire.

00:52:49.680 --> 00:52:57.400
It's a hard life being a dog - long walks, warm beds, tasty treats.

00:52:56.480 --> 00:53:07.240
And, to be honest, that's pretty much Oscar's limit, but, today at the fair, we're going to find out if he can do what his breed is actually supposed to - retrieve.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:10.360
He's already had some expert training.

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:14.960
Now for the ultimate test - head-to-head with his nemesis, Sammy the black Labrador.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:18.000
How are you doing, Siobhan?

00:53:18.640 --> 00:53:20.000
OK.

00:53:19.160 --> 00:53:20.360
Good to see it all happening in this arena.

00:53:19.520 --> 00:53:22.440
Now, you've been competing today, haven't you?

00:53:21.880 --> 00:53:23.200
Yeah.

00:53:22.440 --> 00:53:27.720
I've been doing some scurries and I've been doing the scramble.

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:30.480
Siobhan's won two rosettes today.

00:53:29.920 --> 00:53:33.520
Let's see how a top dog does it.

00:53:33.320 --> 00:53:38.400
So, Oscar, this guy's something of a champion, so pay really close attention, OK?

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:49.480
Good boy.

00:53:48.760 --> 00:53:50.600
Fetch it.

00:53:49.640 --> 00:53:51.000
Good boy.

00:53:50.520 --> 00:53:52.400
Very good.

00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:56.040
With Sammy and Siobhan looking on, now it's our turn.

00:53:55.280 --> 00:53:58.040
Don't let me down, Oscar!

00:53:57.080 --> 00:54:00.160
We've been struggling a little bit with Oscar's training.

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:01.440
The moment of truth.

00:54:02.400 --> 00:54:04.480
Oscar, this is what your half an hour's training has been all about, OK?

00:54:03.520 --> 00:54:05.120
Oscar?

00:54:04.640 --> 00:54:05.880
Right, here we go.

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:06.040
Here we go.

00:54:05.240 --> 00:54:06.560
Look this way.

00:54:05.600 --> 00:54:06.720
Here's the mark.

00:54:05.960 --> 00:54:07.520
Ready?

00:54:06.560 --> 00:54:07.840
That's it.

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:08.800
That's it.

00:54:07.960 --> 00:54:09.560
Go on!

00:54:18.560 --> 00:54:20.320
Go on.

00:54:19.360 --> 00:54:20.480
Yes!

00:54:19.560 --> 00:54:21.320
The green thing, Oscar!

00:54:21.520 --> 00:54:22.560
Oh, dearie me.

00:54:23.240 --> 00:54:25.560
So close.

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:30.080
And that, I have to say, is the difference between half an hour's training and a good two and a half years.

00:54:29.120 --> 00:54:31.880
So, well done, Siobhan, and good luck.

00:54:36.600 --> 00:54:39.880
Oh!

00:54:39.200 --> 00:54:42.440
Well done!

00:54:43.400 --> 00:54:47.080
Hey, Joe, how did Oscar do?

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:52.160
We've had a breakthrough, but I think it's fair to say he's not in danger of winning any trophies here.

00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:52.400
No.

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:53.840
Well, somebody did.

00:54:53.040 --> 00:54:54.080
Look at that!

00:54:53.760 --> 00:54:54.960
Oh, no way!

00:54:54.080 --> 00:54:55.760
You sharpshooter!

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:56.040
Apparently so.

00:54:55.280 --> 00:54:56.480
Apparently, I might have to take it up.

00:54:55.560 --> 00:54:56.840
Oh, well done.

00:54:56.960 --> 00:55:00.840
Well, that's it from this year's CLA Game Fair.

00:55:00.280 --> 00:55:05.040
Join us next week when we'll be in Powys in Mid Wales...

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:07.920
Where Matt will really have his work cut out and I'm meeting some cattle who have a taste for beer.
